# Music Genre Classification Using Machine Learning and Deep Learning

## 1. Project Overview

This project explores applying machine learning and Deep Learning techniques to automatically classify music genres using the GTZAN Music Genre Dataset.

Three classification models were developed and evaluated:

- Random Forest
- Support Vector Machine (SVM)
- Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)

The objective was to investigate how effectively these models classify music into ten different genres using numerical audio features and spectrogram images.

## 2. Project Objectives

1. Explore and preprocess the GTZAN music genre dataset.
2. Analyse relationships among extracted audio features.
3. Develop Random Forest, SVM and CNN classification models.
4. Evaluate and compare the performance of the three models.
5. Identify influential audio features and examine classification errors.

## 3. Dataset Description

The project uses the GTZAN Music Genre Dataset.

The numerical feature dataset contains 9,990 three-second audio segments and 57 numerical audio features.

The ten music genres are:

- Blues
- Classical
- Country
- Disco
- Hip-hop
- Jazz
- Metal
- Pop
- Reggae
- Rock

The dataset also contains spectrogram images used for CNN development.

### Dataset files

- `features_3_sec.csv`
- `features_30_sec.csv`
- `genres_original/`
- `images_original/`

## 4. Data Preprocessing and Exploratory Data Analysis

The following activities were performed:

- Dataset loading and inspection.
- Missing-value and duplicate checks.
- Genre distribution analysis.
- Audio feature correlation analysis.
- Spectrogram image inspection.
- Mapping audio segments and spectrograms to their original song identifiers.

### Preventing Data Leakage

A song-level train-test split was implemented to prevent segments belonging to the same original song from appearing in both training and testing datasets.

The numerical feature dataset was divided into:

- Training: 800 original songs (7,990 audio segments).
- Testing: 200 original songs (2,000 audio segments).

For the CNN, 799 training spectrogram images and 200 testing images were available. One training song did not have a corresponding spectrogram image.

The CNN training images were further divided into training and validation subsets.

## 5. Model Development

### 5.1 Random Forest

A Random Forest classifier was trained using the extracted numerical audio features.

Configuration:

- Number of estimators: 200
- Random state: 42
- Parallel processing: Enabled

Segment-level predictions were aggregated using majority voting to obtain song-level predictions.

### 5.2 Support Vector Machine (SVM)

An SVM classifier using a Radial Basis Function (RBF) kernel was developed.

Configuration:

- Kernel: RBF
- C: 10
- Gamma: Scale
- Feature scaling: StandardScaler

Song-level predictions were obtained through majority voting across audio segments belonging to each original song.

### 5.3 Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)

A CNN was developed to classify spectrogram images.

The architecture included:

- Convolutional layers.
- Max-pooling layers.
- Flattening layer.
- Dense layer with 128 neurons.
- Dropout regularisation.
- Softmax output layer with ten classes.

The spectrogram images were resized to 128 × 128 pixels and normalised.

The model was trained for 20 epochs using a stratified training-validation split and evaluated on 200 unseen test songs.

## 6. Experimental Results

All three models were evaluated using song-level classification accuracy.

| Model | Correct Predictions | Accuracy |
|---|---:|---:|
| SVM | 163/200 | **81.50%** |
| Random Forest | 157/200 | 78.50% |
| CNN | 100/200 | 50.00% |

### Key Findings

- SVM achieved the highest classification accuracy of 81.50%.
- Random Forest achieved 78.50%, performing closely behind SVM.
- CNN achieved 50.00% test accuracy.
- Classical and metal were among the genres classified particularly well by the traditional models.
- CNN learning curves revealed a considerable gap between training and validation performance, indicating overfitting.

Under the experimental conditions used, SVM was the best-performing model.

